I simplified the example as much as I could. "a" is still a function that calls itself and "testing" is a function that calls a, where one parameter is a vector[int i1]. They work separately when "testing" isn't calling "a".

This way it is working: (I only include s, because if no Type is in the function it seems to cause an error. I need to keep the "if else" to give a condition to calling a itself, so that had to stay in it as well.))
template <class Type>
Type a(int i1, double x, Type s)
{
Type result;
if (i1 == 1)
{
result = 1;
}
else
{
result = a(i1 - 1, x, s) + s;

}

return result;
}
template <class Type>

Type testing(int i1, double x, vector<Type> y)
{
Type s = 3.3;
Type m = a(i1, y[i1],s);
//Type m = a(i1, x, s); It works when x is just a double given to the function. If you use this line instead, there are no errors
return m;
}
